This is a concrete manifestation of the two-axis framework (Section~\ref{sec:two_axis}): Axis~1 (connector architecture) determines \emph{whether} pruning is viable; Axis~2 (task complexity) determines \emph{which} scoring method succeeds. CV remains a valid connector diagnostic, but the optimal scoring strategy requires knowledge of both axes.

\paragraph{Position encoding mediates the V-shape recovery.}
The monotonic L2 curve versus the non-monotonic random curve on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long (Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}) reflects an interaction between token selection strategy and positional encoding. L2 norm scoring preserves spatial locality: retained tokens form near-contiguous clusters in the ViT grid, producing continuous position shifts after re-indexing that Gemma's RoPE accommodates smoothly---hence monotonic degradation (86.8\% $\to$ 51.7\% from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.5$). Random selection creates scattered gaps that produce position aliasing after re-indexing. At $k{\approx}0.9$, the aliasing is maximally destructive: a small number of randomly placed gaps create highly irregular inter-token spacing, collapsing SR to 33.6\%. Per-task analysis reveals this aggregate is driven by specific tasks with position-sensitive spatial reasoning: Task~4 (``put mugs on plates'') drops from 80\% at $k{=}0.7$ to 12\% at $k{=}0.85$, contributing disproportionately to the V-shape minimum. At $k{=}0.7$, enough tokens are removed that the surviving positions become approximately uniform again, and SR recovers to 64.6\%---exceeding the $k{=}0.9$ level by ${\sim}2{\times}$. The zero-mask ablation (Section~\ref{sec:zeromask}) provides causal confirmation: zeroing token content while preserving positions yields 98.0\% SR, while physically removing the same tokens collapses to 9.0\%. Position encoding, not information content, is the binding constraint---and scoring method determines whether that constraint is violated.

\paragraph{Per-task reversal within L2-dominant regimes.} The Long $k{=}0.85$ aggregate ($+$33.2\,pp for L2) masks extreme per-task heterogeneity (per-task $\sigma{=}$21.5\,pp). L2 outperforms random on 9 of 10 tasks ($+$16 to $+$52\,pp), but on Task~6---a dual-object spatial-relation task (``place white mug on plate \emph{and} chocolate pudding to the right of plate'')---L2 collapses to 8\% while random achieves 52\% ($-$44\,pp reversal). L2 scoring systematically undervalues tokens encoding spatial relationships, which carry low norms but are critical for relational reasoning. This per-task sign flip within an architecture where L2 dominates overall reinforces that no single scoring method is universally safe: task semantics, not just connector architecture or suite-level statistics, determine optimal strategy.

